1. Product development is a learning process, not a delivery process
“Product development is a learning process, not a delivery process”
In my SAP ABAP role, I have realised that completing a development task is not the only measure of success. Before writing or changing code, it is important to understand the business requirement, study the existing program and process flow, and identify how the change may affect other parts of the system. For example, when a change is requested in an SAP report or enhancement, discussing the requirement with the functional team and analysing the existing logic can reveal gaps that may not be clear from the initial request.
This understanding develops through continuous learning, testing and feedback. A first version of a solution may not address every scenario, but testing it with realistic business cases helps identify what needs to improve. Therefore, I agree that progress should not be measured only by how quickly code is delivered. It should also be measured by how much we learn about the problem, reduce uncertainty and improve the final solution. This approach is equally important when exploring AI-based solutions as part of my MBA learning.
2. Product development should begin with assumptions
“Product development should begin with assumptions”
In SAP development, a requirement often comes with assumptions. For example, we may assume that the business process is clearly understood, that the existing data is accurate, that a particular field will always contain a value, or that a change in one program will not affect other processes. If these assumptions are not checked, they can lead to incorrect outputs, unexpected errors or rework after development.
I have understood that assumptions should be identified and made explicit at the beginning of a task rather than being treated as facts. Before starting development, I should clarify the expected input and output, exception scenarios, data conditions, dependencies and acceptance criteria with the relevant stakeholders. Keeping track of these assumptions also makes it easier to revisit them when new information becomes available. This becomes even more important in AI-related projects, where assumptions about data quality, accuracy and user adoption can strongly influence the outcome.
3. Assumptions should be validated before significant investment
“Assumptions should be validated before significant investment”
I believe assumptions should be tested as early as practical so that time and effort are not spent building a solution based on an incorrect understanding. In an SAP project, this can be done through requirement discussions, reviewing sample data, checking existing program behaviour, preparing a small proof of concept, or testing a change in a development or quality environment before it is moved further.
At the same time, I understand that not every assumption can be validated through discussion alone. Some issues become visible only when a working change is tested with actual data and real business scenarios. For example, a program may work for normal inputs but fail for missing values, unusual data combinations or high data volumes. Therefore, validation should begin early and continue through development and testing. The objective is to identify risks as soon as possible, while recognising that practical implementation and feedback may be needed to validate certain assumptions completely.
4. Separate problem validation from solution validation
“Separate problem validation from solution validation”
This principle reminds me not to jump directly into coding as soon as a requirement is shared. First, I need to understand the actual business problem: what is not working, who is affected, why the issue matters and what outcome the business expects. Only after the problem is clear should I consider the technical solution, such as an ABAP program change, report enhancement, interface adjustment or another approach.
For example, if a user requests a new report, the real need may be to reduce manual effort, improve the visibility of information or correct a gap in an existing process. Simply creating the requested report may not solve the underlying problem. By separating problem validation from solution exploration, I can discuss the requirement more effectively with functional consultants and business users, evaluate alternatives and avoid unnecessary development. This mindset will also help me assess AI and automation opportunities: first establish whether there is a meaningful business problem, and then determine whether AI is an appropriate solution.
